  "articleBody": "Painting a bathroom might seem straightforward, but a few common slip-ups can really impact how long your new paint job lasts. Here are some mistakes clients often make and how to steer clear of them:\n\n1. **Skipping surface prep:** You'd be surprised how many folks just paint right over dirty walls or existing mildew. Always clean your walls, remove any mildew, and repair cracks first. It makes a huge difference.\n2. **Using the wrong caulk:** Standard silicone caulk might seem fine, but if it's not paintable, you'll end up with a mess. Make sure to use paintable caulk for those trim and joint lines.\n3. **Rushing drying times:** Bathrooms are high-moisture areas, and they need extra time for paint to dry and cure. Don't use the shower too soon after painting; ideally, wait overnight or even a few days for the best results.\n4. **Underestimating coats:** Sometimes one coat just isn't enough, especially with color changes or in high-traffic bathrooms. Plan for at least two coats, and be prepared for touch-ups.\n5. **Ignoring the details:** Ceilings, trim, doors, and the area behind the toilet often get overlooked. Make sure your painting plan includes all these spots for a complete, professional look. If these common issues sound familiar, or if you're dealing with extensive mildew or damage, it’s a good idea to chat with a professional remodeler like us for a lasting finish.",
  "datePublished": "2026-08-09"
